{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1786125770199,"sessionID":"ses_022ada93cffeiuPDQc9GVcDNCF","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_00_nSF7gMFhjN2T9a0OM4uk0989","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"echo \"=== directory-tree / prose description of specs location WITHOUT literal .opencode/specs ===\"\nrg -n 'specs/|spec folder|specs directory' -g 'README.md' -g '!**/.worktrees/**' -g '!**/node_modules/**' -g '!specs/**' . 2>/dev/null | rg -v '\\.opencode/specs' | rg -i 'layout|tree|structure|location|under|root|directory' | head -15\necho \"\"\necho \"=== .opencode/skills/README.md: does it describe where specs live in prose? ===\"\nrg -n -i 'spec' .opencode/skills/README.md | head -15","workdir":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public"},"output":"=== directory-tree / prose description of specs location WITHOUT literal .opencode/specs ===\n./README.md:59:1. **Structured documentation** (Spec Kit) - every file change gets a spec folder recording what changed, why and how. Like a lab notebook for software.\n./README.md:198:The Spec Kit enforces structured spec folders for every file-modifying conversation. Gate 3 requires a spec folder answer before any file modification begins (only typo/whitespace fixes under 5 characters are exempt).\n./README.md:1259:A: Gate 3 blocks file modifications until a spec folder answer is provided. You can skip it with option D, but skipped sessions are undocumented and will not be recoverable via memory search. For trivial changes under 5 characters in a single file, Gate 3 does not trigger.\n./README.md:1267:A: Yes. The Spec Kit documentation workflow (Gate 3, spec folders, templates) works independently of the memory MCP server. You lose cross-session memory retrieval, but structured documentation, agent routing and skill loading all still work.\n./.opencode/skills/system-spec-kit/templates/examples/README.md:141:For spec packet validation, run the system-spec-kit validator against the target spec folder rather than this examples directory.\n./.opencode/skills/system-spec-kit/templates/scratch/README.md:24:This directory belongs to the System Spec Kit template set. Update it only when the scratch folder contract changes for all future spec folders.\n./.opencode/skills/system-spec-kit/templates/changelog/README.md:30:`templates/changelog/` contains packet-local changelog templates generated inside spec folders. These templates summarize canonical packet docs and are separate from global release-note templates under `.opencode/changelog/`.\n./.opencode/skills/system-spec-kit/templates/changelog/README.md:34:- `root.md` renders changelogs for root spec folders.\n./.opencode/skills/system-spec-kit/templates/changelog/README.md:106:| `root.md` | Root spec folder changelog generation. |\n./.opencode/skills/system-spec-kit/scripts/validation/README.md:61:- Run these scripts against spec folders or source trees as noted; the audit recurses directories.\n./.opencode/skills/cli-external-orchestration/cli-opencode/benchmark/README.md:59:| [`034-goal-hook-playbooks-and-validation`](../../../../specs/cli-external-orchestration/034-goal-hook-playbooks-and-validation/spec.md) | The spec packet that captured the underlying evidence |\n./.opencode/skills/system-spec-kit/scripts/rules/README.md:3:description: \"Shell rule modules that validate spec folder structure, metadata and documentation state.\"\n./.opencode/bin/README.md:185:The same shared module tracks 7 dist-producing packages total (the 3 CLI shims' own `mcp_server`s plus `system-spec-kit/{shared,scripts}`, `mcp-code-mode/mcp-server`, and `sk-design/sk-design-md-generator/backend`) and backs three other consumers: `validate.sh`'s `run_node_orchestrator()` fails closed (exit `3`, no auto-rebuild) when the compiled spec-validation orchestrator it depends on is stale. the `sk-code` `claude-posttooluse.sh` PostToolUse hook prints a non-blocking `STALE DIST WARNING` banner when an edit lands in a watched source tree. and the `mk-dist-freshness-guard` OpenCode plugin (`.opencode/plugins/`) surfaces stale packages to the agent via bounded system-context injection (`experimental.chat.system.transform`) plus an append-only `.opencode/logs/dist-freshness-guard.log`, never stdout/stderr, which the TUI would paint over the chat input, refreshed before a Bash call matching `opencode run`/`validate.sh` and once per session on `session.created`. Root cause and full design: `system-speckit/028-memory-search-intelligence/002-spec-data-quality/050-validate-sh-dist-freshness-and-repo-remediation/001-dist-freshness-enforcement` spec folder.\n./.opencode/skills/system-spec-kit/scripts/spec/README.md:17:`scripts/spec/` owns shell entrypoints for spec folder lifecycle work. It creates packet folders, upgrades documentation levels, validates structure, checks completion state and archives finished or stale folders.\n./.opencode/skills/system-spec-kit/scripts/spec/README.md:23:- Scripts accept explicit spec folder paths and are intended to run from the repository root.\n\n=== .opencode/skills/README.md: does it describe where specs live in prose? ===\n20:| Aspect | What you get |\n79:| [`system-spec-kit`](system-spec-kit/README.md) | Documentation and memory for AI-assisted development. Every file change gets a spec folder, and the memory daemon answers over MCP or the `spec-memory` CLI |\n156:| Advisor returns an empty list | No skill matched above the threshold, or `SKILL.md` files are missing | Lower the threshold to inspect low-confidence matches: `python3 skill_advisor.py \"query\" --threshold 0.5`. Verify frontmatter with `head -10 .opencode/skills/*/SKILL.md`. |\n157:| Advisor returns the wrong skill | Overly broad description terms in a skill's frontmatter, or a synonym that pulls unrelated skills | Tighten the `description` in that skill's `SKILL.md`. Add specific `trigger_phrases`. Compare native `advisor_recommend` output with the Python shim's to isolate the mismatch. |\n171:Yes. The advisor returns a ranked list. A review task might load `sk-code`'s code-review mode as the primary standard and its router-selected surface evidence as a secondary layer when findings need surface-specific detail. The calling agent decides which sections apply.\n175:Skill-local scripts live in a skill's own `scripts/` directory and handle domain-specific automation. The shared advisor scripts live under `system-skill-advisor/mcp-server/scripts/` and handle routing, graph compilation, benchmarking and regression.\n193:| [`system-spec-kit/README.md`](system-spec-kit/README.md) | Spec folder workflow, memory foundation and context preservation |\n","metadata":{"output":"=== directory-tree / prose description of specs location WITHOUT literal .opencode/specs ===\n./README.md:59:1. **Structured documentation** (Spec Kit) - every file change gets a spec folder recording what changed, why and how. Like a lab notebook for software.\n./README.md:198:The Spec Kit enforces structured spec folders for every file-modifying conversation. Gate 3 requires a spec folder answer before any file modification begins (only typo/whitespace fixes under 5 characters are exempt).\n./README.md:1259:A: Gate 3 blocks file modifications until a spec folder answer is provided. You can skip it with option D, but skipped sessions are undocumented and will not be recoverable via memory search. For trivial changes under 5 characters in a single file, Gate 3 does not trigger.\n./README.md:1267:A: Yes. The Spec Kit documentation workflow (Gate 3, spec folders, templates) works independently of the memory MCP server. You lose cross-session memory retrieval, but structured documentation, agent routing and skill loading all still work.\n./.opencode/skills/system-spec-kit/templates/examples/README.md:141:For spec packet validation, run the system-spec-kit validator against the target spec folder rather than this examples directory.\n./.opencode/skills/system-spec-kit/templates/scratch/README.md:24:This directory belongs to the System Spec Kit template set. Update it only when the scratch folder contract changes for all future spec folders.\n./.opencode/skills/system-spec-kit/templates/changelog/README.md:30:`templates/changelog/` contains packet-local changelog templates generated inside spec folders. These templates summarize canonical packet docs and are separate from global release-note templates under `.opencode/changelog/`.\n./.opencode/skills/system-spec-kit/templates/changelog/README.md:34:- `root.md` renders changelogs for root spec folders.\n./.opencode/skills/system-spec-kit/templates/changelog/README.md:106:| `root.md` | Root spec folder changelog generation. |\n./.opencode/skills/system-spec-kit/scripts/validation/README.md:61:- Run these scripts against spec folders or source trees as noted; the audit recurses directories.\n./.opencode/skills/cli-external-orchestration/cli-opencode/benchmark/README.md:59:| [`034-goal-hook-playbooks-and-validation`](../../../../specs/cli-external-orchestration/034-goal-hook-playbooks-and-validation/spec.md) | The spec packet that captured the underlying evidence |\n./.opencode/skills/system-spec-kit/scripts/rules/README.md:3:description: \"Shell rule modules that validate spec folder structure, metadata and documentation state.\"\n./.opencode/bin/README.md:185:The same shared module tracks 7 dist-producing packages total (the 3 CLI shims' own `mcp_server`s plus `system-spec-kit/{shared,scripts}`, `mcp-code-mode/mcp-server`, and `sk-design/sk-design-md-generator/backend`) and backs three other consumers: `validate.sh`'s `run_node_orchestrator()` fails closed (exit `3`, no auto-rebuild) when the compiled spec-validation orchestrator it depends on is stale. the `sk-code` `claude-posttooluse.sh` PostToolUse hook prints a non-blocking `STALE DIST WARNING` banner when an edit lands in a watched source tree. and the `mk-dist-freshness-guard` OpenCode plugin (`.opencode/plugins/`) surfaces stale packages to the agent via bounded system-context injection (`experimental.chat.system.transform`) plus an append-only `.opencode/logs/dist-freshness-guard.log`, never stdout/stderr, which the TUI would paint over the chat input, refreshed before a Bash call matching `opencode run`/`validate.sh` and once per session on `session.created`. Root cause and full design: `system-speckit/028-memory-search-intelligence/002-spec-data-quality/050-validate-sh-dist-freshness-and-repo-remediation/001-dist-freshness-enforcement` spec folder.\n./.opencode/skills/system-spec-kit/scripts/spec/README.md:17:`scripts/spec/` owns shell entrypoints for spec folder lifecycle work. It creates packet folders, upgrades documentation levels, validates structure, checks completion state and archives finished or stale folders.\n./.opencode/skills/system-spec-kit/scripts/spec/README.md:23:- Scripts accept explicit spec folder paths and are intended to run from the repository root.\n\n=== .opencode/skills/README.md: does it describe where specs live in prose? ===\n20:| Aspect | What you get |\n79:| [`system-spec-kit`](system-spec-kit/README.md) | Documentation and memory for AI-assisted development. Every file change gets a spec folder, and the memory daemon answers over MCP or the `spec-memory` CLI |\n156:| Advisor returns an empty list | No skill matched above the threshold, or `SKILL.md` files are missing | Lower the threshold to inspect low-confidence matches: `python3 skill_advisor.py \"query\" --threshold 0.5`. Verify frontmatter with `head -10 .opencode/skills/*/SKILL.md`. |\n157:| Advisor returns the wrong skill | Overly broad description terms in a skill's frontmatter, or a synonym that pulls unrelated skills | Tighten the `description` in that skill's `SKILL.md`. Add specific `trigger_phrases`. Compare native `advisor_recommend` output with the Python shim's to isolate the mismatch. |\n171:Yes. The advisor returns a ranked list. A review task might load `sk-code`'s code-review mode as the primary standard and its router-selected surface evidence as a secondary layer when findings need surface-specific detail. The calling agent decides which sections apply.\n175:Skill-local scripts live in a skill's own `scripts/` directory and handle domain-specific automation. The shared advisor scripts live under `system-skill-advisor/mcp-server/scripts/` and handle routing, graph compilation, benchmarking and regression.\n193:| [`system-spec-kit/README.md`](system-spec-kit/README.md) | Spec folder workflow, memory foundation and context preservation |\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"echo \"=== directory-tree / prose description of specs location WITHOUT literal .opencode/specs ===\"\nrg -n 'specs/|spec folder|specs directory' -g 'README.md' -g '!**/.worktrees/**' -g '!**/node_modules/**' -g '!specs/**' . 2>/dev/null | rg -v '\\.opencode/specs' | rg -i 'layout|tree|structure|location|under|root|directory' | head -15\necho \"\"\necho \"=== .opencode/skills/README.md: does it describe where specs live in prose? ===\"\nrg -n -i 'spec' .opencode/skills/README.md | head -15","time":{"start":1786125770195,"end":1786125770198}},"id":"prt_fdd6458b2001D65IgY0Qgg1VlW","sessionID":"ses_022ada93cffeiuPDQc9GVcDNCF","messageID":"msg_fdd642a94001VrWYPoG8xqPnVD"}}
