{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1786125780680,"sessionID":"ses_022ada93cffeiuPDQc9GVcDNCF","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_00_VnO4V7pL23sAl8ZsyMAG1365","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"echo \"=== cli-opencode README spec references ===\"\nrg -n 'spec' .opencode/skills/cli-external-orchestration/cli-opencode/README.md | head -12\necho \"\"\necho \"=== cli-opencode benchmark README uses canonical specs/ links? ===\"\nrg -n 'specs/' .opencode/skills/cli-external-orchestration/cli-opencode/benchmark/README.md | head -6\necho \"\"\necho \"=== system-spec-kit templates README any specs-root claim ===\"\nrg -n '\\.opencode/specs|specs/' .opencode/skills/system-spec-kit/templates/README.md | head -8\necho \"\"\necho \"=== check mcp-server hooks README index (hook docs dir) ===\"\nrg -n 'specs/' .opencode/skills/system-spec-kit/mcp-server/hooks/README.md | head -6","workdir":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public"},"output":"=== cli-opencode README spec references ===\n22:| Aspect | What you get |\n41:It does not write application code or manage spec folders. `sk-code` owns code standards and tests. `system-spec-kit` owns spec folders, memory and continuity. cli-opencode dispatches to OpenCode and hands the result back to the caller.\n49:| **Cross-AI handback** | bridges the dispatch result back into the caller's spec folder through the Memory Handback |\n85:You get structured JSON events streamed to stdout as the session runs, ending with a final tool-result message. The `</dev/null` is mandatory on non-interactive runs. Do not pass top-level `--agent` by default: `context`, `review`, `debug` and `ai-council` are subagents that route through a primary path (or, for `ai-council`, through its own `/deep:ai-council` command). Top-level `--agent` is only conditionally allowed for primary agents such as `plan` or `orchestrate` after `opencode run --help` confirms acceptance. When a specific agent profile matters, describe it in the prompt body instead, as this example does.\n94:  \"Run a parallel research branch for the approved spec folder.\" \\\n98:The session runs under its own session id in the shared `~/.local/share/opencode/` database (`opencode.db`, `storage/`, `snapshot/`), inspectable independently via `opencode export <session_id>`. `--share` publishes a browser-accessible URL.\n110:This is what sets cli-opencode apart from every sibling in the cli-* family. A sibling dispatch sends a raw model behind a thin CLI wrapper, with no plugins, no skills, no MCP tools and no memory database. `opencode run` loads the project wholesale. One command gives the dispatched session access to Spec Kit Memory's tool set, the code graph for structural queries, the skill advisor, sequential thinking and every project-specific plugin. That is the reason you reach for this skill when the task needs project context rather than a model alone.\n136:OpenCode resolves project-local agent files from `.opencode/agents/<slug>.md`, but the live dispatch contract is narrower than file presence alone. Subagents such as `context`, `review`, `debug` and the rest of the roster are invoked through a primary path, never as direct specialist `opencode run --agent` slugs. Use `orchestrate` for generic subagent routing when help-verified, omit `--agent` for the default path and never pass `--agent general`. Some small-model paths documented in `SKILL.md` carry narrower omit-agent caveats. The full roster lives in `references/agent-delegation.md`.\n144:Reach for cli-opencode when a task needs the project's full runtime: the memory database, the code graph, every plugin, skill and MCP tool. Use it too for a parallel detached session on ablation or worker-farm work. A non-Anthropic CLI that needs OpenCode as a bridge to a project subsystem such as spec-kit, memory, the code graph and the skill advisor also lands here. Skip it for simple tasks the caller can answer directly, for raw model dispatch where a sibling cli-* is leaner, for the interactive OpenCode TUI and for anything a plain shell command already covers.\n165:| `system-spec-kit` | Owns spec folders, memory and continuity. The Memory Handback bridges an OpenCode dispatch back into the caller's spec folder. |\n183:| Context too large or truncated | The prompt references broad paths instead of specific files | Split large tasks and use `--file` for attachments |\n191:A: A sibling cli-* skill dispatches to a single provider's binary. cli-opencode dispatches into a session that loads every plugin, skill, MCP server and the Spec Kit Memory database for the project. When the task needs the code graph, memory search, a project-specific plugin or the skill advisor, only cli-opencode provides all of them in one shot. When it only needs a model, a sibling is leaner.\n\n=== cli-opencode benchmark README uses canonical specs/ links? ===\n59:| [`034-goal-hook-playbooks-and-validation`](../../../../specs/cli-external-orchestration/034-goal-hook-playbooks-and-validation/spec.md) | The spec packet that captured the underlying evidence |\n\n=== system-spec-kit templates README any specs-root claim ===\n69:│ specs/...        │ ◀─── │ same contract    │\n180:| Exports   | Rendered packet docs are written into `specs/` by `create.sh`, not by this folder directly.                                                          |\n202:│ scaffold writes rendered docs to specs/  │\n237:bash .opencode/skills/system-spec-kit/scripts/spec/validate.sh specs/example --strict\n\n=== check mcp-server hooks README index (hook docs dir) ===\n","metadata":{"output":"=== cli-opencode README spec references ===\n22:| Aspect | What you get |\n41:It does not write application code or manage spec folders. `sk-code` owns code standards and tests. `system-spec-kit` owns spec folders, memory and continuity. cli-opencode dispatches to OpenCode and hands the result back to the caller.\n49:| **Cross-AI handback** | bridges the dispatch result back into the caller's spec folder through the Memory Handback |\n85:You get structured JSON events streamed to stdout as the session runs, ending with a final tool-result message. The `</dev/null` is mandatory on non-interactive runs. Do not pass top-level `--agent` by default: `context`, `review`, `debug` and `ai-council` are subagents that route through a primary path (or, for `ai-council`, through its own `/deep:ai-council` command). Top-level `--agent` is only conditionally allowed for primary agents such as `plan` or `orchestrate` after `opencode run --help` confirms acceptance. When a specific agent profile matters, describe it in the prompt body instead, as this example does.\n94:  \"Run a parallel research branch for the approved spec folder.\" \\\n98:The session runs under its own session id in the shared `~/.local/share/opencode/` database (`opencode.db`, `storage/`, `snapshot/`), inspectable independently via `opencode export <session_id>`. `--share` publishes a browser-accessible URL.\n110:This is what sets cli-opencode apart from every sibling in the cli-* family. A sibling dispatch sends a raw model behind a thin CLI wrapper, with no plugins, no skills, no MCP tools and no memory database. `opencode run` loads the project wholesale. One command gives the dispatched session access to Spec Kit Memory's tool set, the code graph for structural queries, the skill advisor, sequential thinking and every project-specific plugin. That is the reason you reach for this skill when the task needs project context rather than a model alone.\n136:OpenCode resolves project-local agent files from `.opencode/agents/<slug>.md`, but the live dispatch contract is narrower than file presence alone. Subagents such as `context`, `review`, `debug` and the rest of the roster are invoked through a primary path, never as direct specialist `opencode run --agent` slugs. Use `orchestrate` for generic subagent routing when help-verified, omit `--agent` for the default path and never pass `--agent general`. Some small-model paths documented in `SKILL.md` carry narrower omit-agent caveats. The full roster lives in `references/agent-delegation.md`.\n144:Reach for cli-opencode when a task needs the project's full runtime: the memory database, the code graph, every plugin, skill and MCP tool. Use it too for a parallel detached session on ablation or worker-farm work. A non-Anthropic CLI that needs OpenCode as a bridge to a project subsystem such as spec-kit, memory, the code graph and the skill advisor also lands here. Skip it for simple tasks the caller can answer directly, for raw model dispatch where a sibling cli-* is leaner, for the interactive OpenCode TUI and for anything a plain shell command already covers.\n165:| `system-spec-kit` | Owns spec folders, memory and continuity. The Memory Handback bridges an OpenCode dispatch back into the caller's spec folder. |\n183:| Context too large or truncated | The prompt references broad paths instead of specific files | Split large tasks and use `--file` for attachments |\n191:A: A sibling cli-* skill dispatches to a single provider's binary. cli-opencode dispatches into a session that loads every plugin, skill, MCP server and the Spec Kit Memory database for the project. When the task needs the code graph, memory search, a project-specific plugin or the skill advisor, only cli-opencode provides all of them in one shot. When it only needs a model, a sibling is leaner.\n\n=== cli-opencode benchmark README uses canonical specs/ links? ===\n59:| [`034-goal-hook-playbooks-and-validation`](../../../../specs/cli-external-orchestration/034-goal-hook-playbooks-and-validation/spec.md) | The spec packet that captured the underlying evidence |\n\n=== system-spec-kit templates README any specs-root claim ===\n69:│ specs/...        │ ◀─── │ same contract    │\n180:| Exports   | Rendered packet docs are written into `specs/` by `create.sh`, not by this folder directly.                                                          |\n202